<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="year-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="year-title">The company year, in order</h2>
      <p>Time limits below are as reported by secondary guides for typical private companies. Applicability depends on your company, and the {% ext "https://www.mca.gov.in/", "MCA portal" %} is the official source.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Company compliance year, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Reported timing</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Books close and audit</th><td>Financial statements are prepared and audited by an eligible auditor</td><td>Before the AGM</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">AGM</th><td>Members meet, unless the company is exempt from holding one</td><td>Within the time the law allows</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">ADT-1</th><td>Notice of appointing an auditor</td><td>Within 15 days of the AGM, where an appointment is made</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">AOC-4</th><td>Filing financial statements</td><td>Within 30 days of the AGM</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">MGT-7 or MGT-7A</th><td>Annual return. MGT-7A is for small companies and OPCs</td><td>Within 60 days of the AGM</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Director KYC</th><td>Directors' KYC with MCA, under rules that may change</td><td>Check the current rule</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Through the year</th><td>Board meetings, registers, minutes, and event-based filings such as share issues or director changes</td><td>Depends on the event</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <div class="note note--warn section-note"><strong>Check this year's dates at source.</strong> Secondary sources in late 2026 conflict: one reports an MCA circular extending AOC-4 to 31 December 2026 and MGT-7 to 31 January 2027, while another, dated 14 October 2026, reported no extension. We could not read MCA's site to confirm. Do not rely on either without checking.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="llp-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="llp-title">If you are an LLP</h2>
      <p>The recurring forms are different. Sources describe Form 11 as the annual return and Form 8 as the statement of account and solvency, and note an order between them. Tax audit or accounts audit may also apply depending on size, so ask. Confirm dates on the MCA portal.</p>
      <p class="para">A dormant company or LLP may still have filing duties. Stopping business does not stop the calendar by itself.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Who can do what</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Preparing and uploading forms</dt><dd>Often done by a company secretary, CA or their staff.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Certifying a form</dt><dd>Some forms need a practising professional's certification. Secondary sources describe the MGT-8 certificate, where required, as issued by a practising company secretary. Ask which applies to each form.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Auditing financial statements</dt><dd>Only an eligible auditor. See {% routeLink "/services/audit-support/", "audit support" %}.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Signing</dt><dd>Directors or designated partners sign and answer for the filing.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="keep-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="keep-title" class="first-h2">Keeping the year on track</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Fix the AGM date early,</strong> then count back to when the audit and books must be finished.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep a one-page calendar</strong> with each form, the person responsible and the date, and review it quarterly.</li>
        <li><strong>Hold the records yourself.</strong> Minutes, registers, filed forms and acknowledgements should sit with the company.</li>
        <li><strong>Note events as they happen.</strong> A change in directors or capital may start its own clock.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep control of your MCA login</strong> and digital signatures, and know when they expire.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="late">If you have fallen behind</h2>
      <p>Late filings usually attract additional fees, and sources report ₹100 per day for many forms. Long defaults can have more serious results for a company and its directors. Do not assume that a small company goes unnoticed. Ask a professional to list what is pending, what it may cost, and whether any official scheme is currently open.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">Questions to ask a provider</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Which forms apply to us this year, and why?</li>
        <li>Who is certifying them, with what qualification and membership number?</li>
        <li>What is your cut-off for receiving audited accounts?</li>
        <li>Are event-based filings and registers included, or only annual forms?</li>
        <li>Will I get copies of every filing and acknowledgement?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fees">What affects the fee</h2>
      <p>Entity type, pending years, event-based filings, secretarial records, and certification. Government fees and additional fees are separate from any professional's charge, so ask for both in writing.</p>

